\section{Introduction}
Adonara (ISO 639-3 adr) is spoken on Adonara Island, East Flores Regency, Indonesia. Linguists treat it as part of the Lamaholot dialect cluster (Central Malayo-Polynesian), and its classification is debated. Most scholarship covers Lamaholot varieties generally. Little is specific to Adonara.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes specific to Adonara were found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Michels, M. (2017), \emph{Western Lamaholot: a cross-dialectal grammar sketch}, Leiden MA thesis (open access; it is the source cited by Grambank for Adonara): \url{https://openaccess.leidenuniv.nl/bitstream/handle/1887/52580/Western%20Lamaholot%20%28definitief%29.pdf?sequence=1}
\item Grambank entry for Adonara: \url{https://grambank.clld.org/languages/adon1237}
\item Wikipedia, ``Adonara language'': \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adonara_language}
\item Wikipedia, ``Lamaholot language'': \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamaholot_language}
\item ``Variasi Dialek Bahasa Lamaholot di Pulau Adonara, Kabupaten Flores Timur: Sebuah Kajian Sinkronik'' (Indonesian-language study of dialect variation on Adonara): \url{https://ojs.unm.ac.id/ijses/article/view/22053}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in the search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found in the search results. No dedicated Adonara dictionary was found. The search summaries mention a mini dictionary only for the Lewotobi dialect of Lamaholot, which is a different variety.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is stable with many speakers but is not known to be taught in schools. Existing documentation is a grammar sketch of western Lamaholot dialects and a dialect-variation study. A curriculum would first need a lexicon, an agreed orthography and local teacher input. No local organisations were identified in the search.

\end{document}
